---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
// Personalized by the operator. Contact email is a placeholder until the site
// mailbox is set up (tracked in OWNER-GUIDE.md).
---
<BaseLayout title="About" description="Who writes this guide.">
  <div class="wrap">
    <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: 'About' }]} />
    <article class="prose" style="margin-top: 1.2rem;">
      <p class="kicker">About</p>
      <h1>About this guide</h1>

      <p>
        I've spent more than fifteen years in fundraising, using many of the
        tools reviewed here. Most of that time has been in and around
        education and the arts, where budgets are tight and a software
        decision follows you for years.
      </p>
      <p>
        This site organizes fundraising software by shop size, because that is
        what actually determines what you should run. A $400k shop and a $6M
        shop need different stacks, and a recommendation that ignores your
        budget isn't a recommendation.
      </p>

      <h2>How the guide works</h2>
      <p>
        Every price on this site comes from the vendor's published pricing or,
        where pricing is quoted per organization, from ranges vendors and their
        customers report. Every recommendation names the shop size and situation
        it fits. Free tools are covered alongside paid ones, and the strongest
        advice on most pages is the simplest: shortlist two or three tools, book
        the demos, and test each one against your own data. A good demo will
        show you things no review can.
      </p>

      <p>
        Spotted a stale price or a recommendation that missed the mark?
        <a href="mailto:hello@nonprofitsoftwareguide.com">Tell me</a>.
        Reader corrections are what keep a guide like this accurate.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
